---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title = "Install Sidecar — the full guide";
const description =
  "The detailed, step-by-step installation guide for Sidecar on macOS: the companion app, the browser extension, and choosing how it answers — your own Claude or Codex subscription, or a private local-chat model set up on your own Mac.";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Base title={title} description={description} active="setup">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Full installation guide</p>
      <h1>Install Sidecar, step by step.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Everything, in order — the companion app, the browser extension, and the one
        choice that makes it answer. In a hurry? The <a href={`${base}setup.html`}>5-minute setup</a>
        is the short version; this page has every detail and every "what if."</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Before you start -->
  <section class="alt" style="padding-top:36px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head"><p class="eyebrow">Before you start</p><h2>Two things you need.</h2></div>
      <ul class="kf-points" style="max-width:70ch">
        <li><b>An Apple Silicon Mac</b> (M1 or newer), on <b>macOS 15 or later</b>. This build doesn't
          run on an Intel Mac.</li>
        <li><b>A way for it to answer</b> — either <b>Claude Code</b> or <b>Codex</b> already installed
          and signed in (with your own subscription), <b>or</b> set up <b>local chat</b> — a private
          model on your own Mac, no account — from inside the panel once Sidecar is running. You set
          this up in step 3; you don't need it before you start.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Step 1: the companion app -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head"><p class="eyebrow">Part 1</p><h2>The companion app.</h2>
        <p>A small app that lives in your menu bar (the icons at the top-right of your screen) and runs
          your AI backend. No terminal or developer tools needed.</p></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><h3>Download the <code>.dmg</code></h3><p>Use the link you were given. If your
          browser warns the file "couldn't be scanned for viruses" (it's a bit large), that's normal —
          choose <b>Keep</b> / <b>Download anyway</b>.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Open it and drag Sidecar to Applications</h3><p>Double-click the
          <code>.dmg</code>. A window opens with the <b>Sidecar</b> app next to an <b>Applications</b>
          shortcut. Drag Sidecar onto that shortcut to copy it where it belongs.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Open Sidecar from your Applications folder</h3><p>Not from the window that
          popped up. (Opening it from the temporary window blocks "Start at login" later, which needs a
          permanent home.) You can then eject the "Sidecar" disk from the Finder sidebar.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Tell macOS you trust it — once</h3><p>Because Sidecar isn't from the App
          Store, the first launch is blocked with <i>"Apple could not verify…"</i>. Open <b>System
          Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</b>, scroll to <b>Security</b>, click <b>Open Anyway</b> next
          to the Sidecar line, and confirm. Open Sidecar once more and click <b>Open</b>. macOS remembers
          — you only do this once. (It goes away entirely once the app is paid-notarized.)</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="fig-note">When it's running you'll see the Sidecar icon in your menu bar. Click it for
        <b>running / stopped</b> status, a <b>Start at login</b> toggle (off until you choose it), and
        <b>Quit</b>. Leave it running while you use the panel in your browser.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Step 2: the extension -->
  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head"><p class="eyebrow">Part 2</p><h2>The browser extension.</h2>
        <p>The panel you actually chat in — for Chrome or Brave.</p></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><h3>Add it to your browser</h3><p>Install from the Chrome Web Store, or load
          the unpacked build you were given — both work in Chrome and Brave. Chrome will warn the
          extension can "read and change all your data on all websites"; that's what lets it read the
          page you ask about, and only while you're actively chatting about it — never in the background.
          Choose <b>Add extension</b>.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Open the side panel</h3><p>Click the Sidecar toolbar icon, or press
          <b>Cmd+Shift+Y</b> (<b>Ctrl+Shift+Y</b> on Windows/Linux) — the same shortcut closes it. If the
          shortcut does nothing, another extension may use that key; set your own at
          <code>chrome://extensions/shortcuts</code>.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><h3>Let it connect</h3><p>The panel looks for the companion app on your own
          computer and connects. The header shows a green dot and <b>App connected</b> when it's talking
          to the app. If it can't connect, make sure the menu-bar app (Part 1) is running — the panel
          retries every couple of seconds on its own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="shot-fig">
        <div class="status-shot"><img src={`${base}assets/screenshots/status-connected.png`} width={1536} height={224} loading="lazy" alt="The top of the Sidecar panel: a green dot with 'App connected', and the find, new-chat, and settings icons." /></div>
        <figcaption>Connected — the green dot and "App connected" mean the panel found the companion app.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="fig-note"><b>Loaded unpacked?</b> To update, replace the files in the same folder and
        press <b>Reload</b> on the extension's card in <code>chrome://extensions</code> (your settings
        are kept). When the Web Store version is available, install it and <b>remove the unpacked one</b>,
        or you'll get two Sidecar panels.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Step 3: choose how it answers -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head"><p class="eyebrow">Part 3</p><h2>Choose how it answers.</h2>
        <p>Sidecar itself doesn't include an AI — you point it at one. Pick either route; you can switch
          any time in <b>Settings → Backend</b>, and choose per message from the composer's model menu.</p></div>

      <figure class="shot-fig">
        <div class="status-shot"><img src={`${base}assets/screenshots/backend-picker.png`} width={844} height={510} loading="lazy" alt="Settings → Backend with three choices: Claude Code (your Claude subscription), Codex (your ChatGPT subscription), and Pi (local) — a local model on your computer, private and offline. A note explains Claude and Codex use your own subscription, while Pi runs on this computer and needs no account." /></div>
        <figcaption><b>Settings → Backend</b> — pick who answers. "Pi (local)" is local chat.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="two-routes">
        <article class="route">
          <p class="route-tag">Route A · your subscription</p>
          <h3>Claude Code or Codex</h3>
          <p>Answer with your own paid assistant — <b>Claude Code</b> (your Claude subscription) or
            <b>Codex</b> (your ChatGPT subscription). Install and sign in to whichever one you'll use —
            you don't need both — then choose it in <b>Settings → Backend</b>. Sidecar's environment
            panel says plainly if neither is found.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="route route-star">
          <p class="route-tag">Route B · fully local</p>
          <h3>Local chat — no account</h3>
          <p>Answer with a private model running entirely on your Mac — nothing sent online, no
            subscription. In <b>Settings</b>, open the <b>Set up local chat</b> card and click the button;
            Sidecar downloads and manages everything for you, a step at a time:</p>
          <ul class="kf-points" style="margin-top:12px">
            <li>a small <b>assistant</b> helper (~30&nbsp;MB),</li>
            <li>the <b>engine</b> that runs models (Ollama, ~160&nbsp;MB), and</li>
            <li>an <b>AI model</b> sized to your Mac — <b>qwen3.5</b> (open, Apache-2.0, reads images
              too), a few GB (the default is about 6.6&nbsp;GB).</li>
          </ul>
          <p style="margin-top:12px">It checks your free disk first and shows progress. When it's ready,
            pick <b>Pi (local)</b> in <b>Settings → Backend</b>. Already run your own Ollama? Sidecar
            detects it and skips the engine step. <a href={`${base}local-chat.html`}>How local chat works →</a></p>
          <figure class="shot-fig">
            <div class="status-shot"><img src={`${base}assets/screenshots/local-chat-setup.png`} width={844} height={456} loading="lazy" alt="The 'Set up local chat' card: a three-step plan — Assistant, Engine, and AI model (qwen3.5:9b, about 6.6 GB), each marked 'Will download' — with a 'Set up local chat' button." /></div>
            <figcaption>One click sets up all three parts; steps you already have are skipped.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Power user -->
  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head"><p class="eyebrow">Optional</p><h2>Power-user / headless.</h2></div>
      <p style="max-width:70ch;color:var(--soft)">Comfortable in a terminal, or running on a machine with
        no desktop? The background program ships <b>inside the app</b> and needs nothing else installed —
        run it directly and there's no menu-bar app:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>/Applications/Sidecar.app/Contents/Resources/sidecar-daemon</code></pre>
      <p style="max-width:70ch;color:var(--soft)">If macOS blocks the inner binary, clear the download
        flag once with <code>xattr -d com.apple.quarantine …</code> on that path. To start it at login
        from the command line, run <code>sidecar-daemon enable-autostart</code> (and
        <code>disable-autostart</code> / <code>autostart-status</code>). Use either this <i>or</i> the
        menu-bar app's "Start at login," not both — turning on the app's version removes this one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Troubleshooting -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head"><p class="eyebrow">If something's off</p><h2>Quick fixes.</h2></div>
      <ul class="kf-points" style="max-width:72ch">
        <li><b>Panel says it can't reach the app.</b> Check the Sidecar icon is in your menu bar (Part 1),
          and give it a couple of seconds — the panel reconnects on its own.</li>
        <li><b>The keyboard shortcut does nothing.</b> Another extension likely owns that key — set your
          own at <code>chrome://extensions/shortcuts</code>.</li>
        <li><b>It won't answer.</b> You haven't finished Part 3 yet — sign in to Claude Code or Codex, or
          set up local chat.</li>
        <li><b>Two Sidecar panels.</b> You have both the unpacked and Web Store extensions installed —
          remove one.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .lead { max-width: 62ch; }
    .fig-note { max-width: 72ch; margin: 18px 0 0; color: var(--faint); font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.55; }
    .fig-note code, .step code, .kf-points code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12.5px;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 10%, transparent); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 6px; color: var(--accent-ink); }

    .shot-fig { margin: 22px 0 0; }
    .shot-fig figcaption { margin-top: 8px; color: var(--faint); font-size: 13.5px; }

    .two-routes { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; align-items: start; }
    @media (max-width: 820px) { .two-routes { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
    .route { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 16px; padding: 22px 22px 20px; }
    .route-star { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 45%, var(--border));
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 6%, var(--surface)); }
    .route-tag { font-family: var(--body); font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: .1em;
      text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); margin: 0 0 4px; }
    .route h3 { font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 21px; margin: 0 0 8px; color: var(--ink); }
    .route p { color: var(--soft); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; }
    .route a { color: var(--accent-ink); }

    .code { background: var(--surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px 14px; margin: 14px 0; overflow-x: auto; }
    .code code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink); }
  </style>
</Base>
